archivedArticles[37]="Artificial intelligence is increasingly becoming an answer machine. It can summarize a report, suggest a diagnosis, rank job applicants, recommend an investment strategy, or draft a response to a customer. In many situations, this is genuinely useful. A task that once required an hour may take a few minutes. Information that was difficult to organize becomes easier to compare. But as AI lowers the cost of producing an answer, it may also create a less obvious problem: people may become less willing to question the answer they receive.\n\nPsychologists have long studied a tendency called automation bias. When a machine provides a recommendation, people can give that recommendation more weight than it deserves, especially when the system is usually reliable. This does not mean that humans blindly obey every computer. In fact, people sometimes distrust algorithms after seeing them make even a small mistake. The deeper point is that our relationship with automated advice is unstable. We may reject useful systems too quickly in some situations and rely on them too heavily in others.\n\nWhy does overreliance happen? One reason is friction. In an earlier lesson, we distinguished between execution friction and decision friction. AI removes enormous amounts of execution friction: it searches, calculates, summarizes, and drafts. That is often beneficial. Yet it can also remove decision friction—the small pause in which a person asks, “Does this make sense?” If checking an answer requires effort while accepting it requires one click, the design of the system quietly changes the incentives around verification.\n\nConsider a financial analyst using an AI tool to summarize a company’s quarterly results. The summary may correctly report that revenue increased by 15 percent. If the analyst stops there, however, an important question may disappear: increased compared with what? Perhaps investors expected 25 percent growth. As we learned when discussing market expectations, good news is not the same as a positive surprise. AI can provide the first-order fact, but the user still has to identify the relevant benchmark and interpret the second-order meaning.\n\nThis distinction matters because many real decisions are not simply information-retrieval problems. They are judgment problems. A judgment problem involves uncertainty, competing goals, incomplete evidence, or consequences that cannot be captured by one metric. Hiring is a good example. An AI system might rank candidates according to predicted performance, but managers still need to ask what “performance” means, how it was measured, and whether historical data contain biases that should not be reproduced. A precise score can create an illusion of objectivity even when the underlying measurement is imperfect.\n\nThis connects directly to Goodhart’s Law, which we discussed in the lesson on busyness and productivity: when a measure becomes a target, it can stop being a good measure. AI systems are often trained or evaluated using measurable proxies because reality itself is too complicated to put into a dataset. A customer-service model might optimize response time, for example, even though a fast response is not necessarily a helpful one. Once an organization begins trusting the score, workers may adapt their behavior to improve the metric rather than the real outcome.\n\nThere is also a feedback problem. Suppose an AI hiring system recommends candidates who resemble people the company previously hired. Managers accept most recommendations because reviewing every application manually is expensive. Those selected candidates then become the new training data. Over time, the system receives more evidence that people similar to its earlier choices are “successful.” The recommendation influences reality, and that changed reality later appears to confirm the recommendation. This is similar to the self-fulfilling dynamics we saw when studying predictions and social proof.\n\nNone of this means that human judgment is automatically superior. Humans are inconsistent, tired, emotional, and vulnerable to biases of their own. A doctor may overlook an unusual diagnosis. A recruiter may favor a candidate who feels familiar. An investor may become attached to a losing position because of loss aversion. The useful comparison, therefore, is not “perfect human versus imperfect machine.” It is one imperfect decision-maker interacting with another.\n\nThe strongest systems may be those that divide responsibilities carefully. AI is particularly valuable when it can process large amounts of information, identify patterns, or generate possible explanations. Humans remain important when the task requires choosing goals, evaluating unusual circumstances, understanding social context, or accepting responsibility for consequences. The boundary will move as technology improves, but the need to define that boundary will not disappear.\n\nDesign can help. Instead of presenting one confident answer, an AI system might show uncertainty, alternative explanations, or the evidence behind a recommendation. In high-stakes situations, it might deliberately introduce useful decision friction by asking the user to confirm an assumption or review a conflicting piece of evidence. This sounds less convenient, but convenience is not always the correct objective. As we saw in the previous lesson, good design does not eliminate every obstacle; it distinguishes wasteful friction from friction that improves a decision.\n\nOrganizations also need to measure whether AI improves outcomes rather than merely whether people use it. Adoption is an easy metric: How many employees opened the tool? How many tasks were completed with AI? But these numbers can become misleading proxies. A better evaluation might compare error rates, decision quality, time saved, and the kinds of mistakes humans and machines make together. Measurement should follow the real goal, not replace it.\n\nAt the individual level, a useful habit is to treat AI output as a proposal rather than a conclusion. Before accepting an important answer, ask three questions. First, what evidence would change this answer? Second, what assumption is doing the most work? Third, what would happen if the answer were wrong? These questions are especially valuable when the output feels polished, because fluency can be mistaken for reliability.\n\nThe economic significance of this skill may grow as AI becomes more abundant. Earlier, we explored the idea that when one capability becomes cheap and widely available, value moves toward what remains scarce. If generating summaries, forecasts, and drafts becomes easy, then producing an answer may become less valuable by itself. The scarce capability may instead be judgment: knowing which question to ask, which evidence matters, when to distrust a confident result, and who should remain accountable.\n\nAI may therefore change expertise in a surprising way. Experts will not necessarily be the people who can produce answers fastest. Machines may win that competition. Expertise may increasingly involve knowing when an apparently good answer is incomplete. The future of human judgment is not about refusing automation. It is about using automation without allowing convenience, confidence, or scale to replace thought.";
archivedSummaries[37]="这篇文章讨论的不是“AI 会不会取代人类判断”这种简单二分法，而是一个更隐蔽的问题：当 AI 把获得答案的执行成本降得很低时，它也可能同时降低人们主动核实答案的意愿。文章用 automation bias 解释这种过度依赖，并把它与上一课的 friction 框架连接起来：AI 消除了大量有益的执行摩擦，但如果连“停下来问一句这合理吗”的决策摩擦也一起消失，便利就可能以牺牲验证为代价。金融分析的例子进一步说明，AI 可以给出“营收增长 15%”这样的第一阶事实，但真正的判断仍需要寻找 benchmark、比较市场预期，并理解第二阶含义。\n\n文章随后把问题推进到 measurement 与 feedback。招聘评分、客服响应时间等看似精确的指标可能只是现实目标的 proxy；一旦组织开始围绕代理指标优化，就会出现 Goodhart’s Law。更进一步，如果 AI 的推荐影响了谁被录用、哪些数据被继续收集，新数据又反过来训练系统，就可能形成自我强化的反馈回路。因此，机器并非天然客观，人类也并非天然可靠；更有价值的问题是如何划分两者的责任边界。\n\n文章最后提出一个很适合迁移到以后 AI 话题的核心框架：Suggestion → Reduced Friction → Trust → Reduced Verification → Feedback。面对重要 AI 输出，可以追问三个问题：什么证据会改变这个答案？哪个假设承担了最多推理工作？如果答案错了会发生什么？当生成答案越来越廉价和普及时，真正稀缺的能力可能从“快速给出答案”转向 judgment：提出正确的问题、识别关键证据、知道何时不该相信一个流畅而自信的结果，并为最终决策承担责任。";


