archivedArticles[45]="Imagine that you are deciding whether a new study method works. You try it before an exam and receive a high score. The result feels convincing: the method must be effective. A week later, you hear that a friend used the same method and also improved. Now you feel even more certain. But perhaps you had already studied more hours, the second exam was easier, and your friend was unusually motivated. Evidence that agrees with an existing belief often feels stronger than evidence that challenges it.\n\nThis tendency is commonly called confirmation bias. Once people have a hypothesis, preference, or expectation, they may search for information that supports it, interpret ambiguous evidence in its favor, and remember confirming examples more easily. The bias does not require dishonesty. A person can sincerely believe they are being objective while their attention is being guided by what they already expect to find.\n\nPrior beliefs are not themselves a problem. In fact, reasoning would be impossible without them. We constantly use past experience to form expectations: dark clouds suggest rain, a familiar brand suggests a certain level of quality, and a strong academic record may suggest that a student is well prepared. The problem begins when a prior belief stops functioning as a starting point and becomes a filter that prevents meaningful updating.\n\nConsider online research. Suppose someone believes that coffee is extremely beneficial for health. If they search “benefits of coffee,” they have already shaped the evidence they are likely to encounter. Search engines may return relevant pages, but relevance is defined partly by the question. Someone searching “risks of coffee” may enter a very different information environment. Both people can spend an hour “doing research” while mainly collecting evidence for the position they began with.\n\nThis creates search selection. Before we evaluate evidence, we choose which evidence enters the room. That selection can matter as much as later interpretation. In #41, we discussed the value of information and asked whether another piece of information could realistically change a decision. Today we add a prior question: are we searching in places where disconfirming information is even allowed to appear?\n\nInterpretation creates another layer. The same mixed result can be explained differently depending on expectations. If a company launches a product and sales rise by 3 percent, an enthusiastic manager may call the launch successful. A skeptical manager may point out that the company expected 10 percent growth. Neither statement changes the observed number. What changes is the benchmark and the causal story attached to it.\n\nThis connects to measurement. Data do not interpret themselves. Analysts choose time periods, comparison groups, definitions, and metrics. A politician, investor, company, or ordinary consumer can emphasize the measurement that makes a preferred story look strongest. That does not mean all interpretations are equally valid. It means good analysis must examine why one metric was selected and what alternative measurements might show.\n\nCausality makes the challenge harder. Suppose a city introduces a new policy and crime falls the following year. Supporters may treat the decline as proof that the policy worked; opponents may attribute it to broader economic conditions. The observed correlation alone cannot settle the disagreement. If people begin with different beliefs, each side can construct a plausible explanation that protects its original position.\n\nConfirmation bias therefore interacts with motivated reasoning. Motivated reasoning occurs when a desired conclusion influences how evidence is processed. The motivation can be financial, social, emotional, or connected to identity. If admitting that a project is failing threatens someone's reputation, they have an incentive to interpret weak signals optimistically. If a belief is shared by a close social group, changing one's mind may also carry a social cost.\n\nThere is a useful connection to #44. Social proof asks how independent the people in a crowd really are. Confirmation bias asks how independent our evidence is from our prior belief. If I choose the search terms, sources, examples, and metrics because they support my expectation, then ten pieces of evidence may not represent ten independent opportunities to learn. They may be ten outputs of the same selection process.\n\nA stronger approach is to search for falsification. Instead of asking only, “What evidence supports my idea?”, ask, “What evidence would make me change my mind?” This question forces a belief to become testable. If no possible result could count against a claim, the claim is protected from evidence rather than supported by it.\n\nOne practical method is a pre-mortem. Before making an important decision, imagine that the decision has failed and ask what most likely caused the failure. A team excited about a new product might list weak demand, technical delays, competitor responses, or excessive costs. The exercise creates space for negative evidence before people become committed to defending the project.\n\nAnother method is to define decision thresholds in advance. An investor might decide before buying an asset what evidence would cause them to sell. A company can specify what performance would count as an unsuccessful experiment before seeing the results. Predefined criteria reduce the temptation to move the goalposts after the outcome becomes known.\n\nThis connects directly to incentives and sunk costs from #42. Once people invest money, time, or reputation in a decision, admitting error becomes more expensive psychologically. The belief is no longer merely an idea; it is connected to past action. That can turn evidence evaluation into self-protection. Separating “Was the original decision reasonable?” from “Is continuing still reasonable now?” helps preserve the ability to update.\n\nFeedback loops can make the problem larger at scale. Recommendation systems learn what users click and show them more similar material. Users then encounter more evidence consistent with their interests, which produces more clicks and further personalization. The system does not need to know anyone's ideology or intention. A simple engagement loop can gradually narrow the range of information a person sees.\n\nThe broader lesson is that rational thinking is not the absence of beliefs. It is the ability to update beliefs when the evidence changes. Good judgment therefore depends partly on information architecture: how we search, which comparisons we use, when we set criteria, and whether disagreement can enter before commitment becomes too strong.\n\nSo the next time evidence seems overwhelmingly supportive, try one additional question: “What would I expect to see if I were wrong?” Then look for it. Confidence should come not only from finding evidence that fits a belief, but from giving the belief a serious chance to fail and watching it survive.";
archivedSummaries[45]="今天讨论 confirmation bias，但重点不是“人喜欢听自己想听的”这么简单，而是把它拆成一个 information architecture 问题：Prior Belief 会影响 Search → Interpretation → Memory → Updating。也就是说，在我们开始“分析证据”之前，证据池本身可能已经被选择过。\n\n第一层是 search selection。搜索“coffee benefits”和“coffee risks”的人都可能认真研究一小时，但进入的是不同 evidence set。因此 #41 的 Value of Information 要再加一道门：不仅问“下一条信息能不能改变决定”，还要问“我的搜索方式有没有允许反例出现？”\n\n第二层接 measurement 和 causality。同一个 3% 增长，可以因为 benchmark 不同被解释成成功或失败；同一个政策后犯罪率下降，也不能只凭时间先后证明因果。Data do not interpret themselves——指标、比较组和因果机制都需要被检查。\n\n第三层接 #44 的 Independence Check：群体意见可能不独立，我们自己的 evidence 也可能不独立。如果十条证据都是由同一个 prior belief 通过搜索词、来源和指标筛出来的，它们并不是十次真正独立的学习机会。\n\n今天的新工具是 Disconfirmation Test：不要只问“What supports my belief?”，而要问“What evidence would make me change my mind?”。再配合 pre-mortem 和 predefined thresholds，可以在 sunk cost、identity 和 reputation 介入之前保留更新空间。\n\n核心不是永远怀疑，而是 calibration：让 confidence 与 evidence quality 相匹配。";


