articles.push({
  "n": 46,
  "title": "Why the Default Option Is So Powerful",
  "cat": "Behavioral Economics",
  "framework": "Default → Lower Immediate Friction → Inaction → Repeated Choice → Status Quo Reinforcement",
  "idea": "A default does not remove choice, but it changes the cost of doing nothing. Because attention, effort, and certainty are scarce, the option selected automatically can shape behavior far more than its apparent neutrality suggests."
});
archivedArticles[46]="Imagine starting a new job and receiving a form about retirement savings. One box says that 5 percent of your salary will automatically go into a retirement account unless you choose a different amount. You are free to change it, reduce it to zero, or increase it. Yet many employees simply leave the box as it is. Now imagine the opposite system: nothing is saved unless you actively enroll. The economic options may be almost identical, but participation can look very different.\n\nThis is the power of a default. A default is the option that takes effect when a person does nothing. It appears in pension plans, software settings, subscriptions, privacy controls, organ-donation systems, delivery preferences, and countless digital services. Defaults are interesting because they do not necessarily restrict freedom. People may still be allowed to choose anything. What changes is the path of least resistance.\n\nWhy should such a small design choice matter? One reason is friction. Taking action requires time and attention. Even a simple decision may involve opening a page, understanding unfamiliar terms, remembering a password, or comparing several alternatives. Each step is minor, but minor steps accumulate. When the expected benefit of changing a setting feels uncertain, doing nothing becomes attractive.\n\nThis connects directly to scarcity. Attention is limited, and most people face many decisions every day. A worker may care about retirement savings but also need to answer emails, pay bills, plan dinner, and solve immediate problems. The default effectively says, “If you do not spend attention here, this is what will happen.” It therefore allocates outcomes partly according to where people do not focus.\n\nInertia is another mechanism. Once an option is already in place, changing it can feel like an active decision while keeping it feels passive. This difference matters because action creates a sense of responsibility. If I switch investment funds and performance becomes worse, I may blame myself. If I leave the existing fund unchanged and it performs badly, the outcome can feel less directly caused by me. Staying with the default may therefore reduce anticipated regret.\n\nDefaults can also act as recommendations. People sometimes assume that the organization designing the system has chosen the default for a reason. A new employee may think the standard savings rate is what financial experts consider appropriate. A user may interpret a privacy setting as normal or safe because the platform preselected it. In situations of uncertainty, the default becomes not only an easy option but also an informational signal.\n\nBehavioral economists often discuss the default effect alongside status quo bias, the tendency to prefer the current state simply because it is current. The two ideas overlap but are not identical. A default is a feature of the environment; status quo bias describes a pattern in behavior. A well-designed default can take advantage of inertia, but people may also remain with an existing option for rational reasons, such as switching costs or genuine satisfaction.\n\nThat distinction matters for causality. Suppose 80 percent of customers keep a default electricity plan. We cannot immediately conclude that 80 percent are psychologically biased. Perhaps alternative plans are genuinely worse, switching is complicated, or customers have already compared them. Observing persistence tells us what people did, not why they did it. To identify the default effect, researchers need a credible comparison between similar groups facing different starting options.\n\nThe ethics of defaults depend heavily on alignment. If a default helps people achieve goals they already have but often fail to act on, it can reduce harmful friction. Automatic retirement enrollment is a common example: many people say they want to save for the future, yet enrollment paperwork can delay action. A sensible default can convert intention into behavior without eliminating the right to opt out.\n\nBut defaults can also exploit inattention. A free trial that automatically becomes a paid subscription uses the same basic mechanism. The customer can cancel, but the company benefits when people forget, postpone, or find cancellation annoying. Here the default may be aligned with the seller's revenue rather than the user's original goal. The design still preserves formal choice, but the practical burden is unevenly distributed.\n\nThis introduces an important distinction between formal freedom and effective freedom. Formal freedom asks whether an option is legally or technically available. Effective freedom asks how costly it is to exercise that option in practice. If cancelling a service requires six screens, a phone call, and a long wait while joining requires one click, the two choices are not equally frictionless even if both are technically possible.\n\nMeasurement can reveal this asymmetry. Instead of asking only how many customers remain subscribed, we might examine cancellation attempts, time required to leave, complaints, accidental renewals, and how behavior changes when cancellation becomes easier. A high retention rate can look like customer satisfaction, but it may partly reflect friction. This is another Goodhart-style warning: a visible outcome can become misleading when we ignore the mechanism producing it.\n\nDefaults also create second-order effects over time. Consider a workplace savings plan. Automatic enrollment may increase the number of employees who save, which is a first-order effect. But if the default contribution rate is too low, some employees may treat it as a recommendation and save less than they otherwise would have. The same design can therefore increase participation while anchoring contribution levels. Improving one metric does not guarantee that the entire system is optimized.\n\nA useful analytical question is therefore: who benefits from inaction? When people do nothing, some outcome still occurs. Money may remain in an account, a subscription may renew, data may be shared, or an employee may remain outside a savings plan. The default determines who receives the advantage when attention is absent.\n\nThis question connects to incentives. Organizations choose defaults, and organizations have objectives. Sometimes those objectives align with users; sometimes they conflict. A company that earns money from renewal has an incentive to make continuation easy. A pension administrator may have an incentive to increase participation. Understanding the designer's incentive does not prove manipulation, but it helps identify which direction the architecture is likely to push.\n\nThe broader lesson is that doing nothing is still a decision pathway. Inaction does not produce a neutral outcome; it produces the outcome chosen by the system's architecture. Once we see that, defaults become easier to analyze. We can ask what friction they remove, what information they signal, whose incentives they serve, and what happens over time.\n\nSo when an option is already selected for you, the most useful question may not be “Can I change this?” It may be: “What happens if I do nothing, and who designed that outcome?” That question turns an invisible starting point into something we can actually evaluate.";
archivedSummaries[46]="今天讨论 default effect，但重点不是“人懒所以不改默认选项”，而是把 default 看成一种对 inaction outcome 的设计：当你什么都不做时，系统仍然必须决定会发生什么。于是 default 的真正力量来自 Default → Lower Immediate Friction → Inaction → Repeated Choice → Status Quo Reinforcement。\n\n第一层接回 friction 与 attention scarcity。改变设置需要时间、理解和行动，而保持默认值几乎不消耗注意力。因此 default 实际上是在分配“当注意力缺席时谁得到什么结果”。\n\n第二层加入重要 distinction：formal freedom ≠ effective freedom。一个选项技术上“可以退出”，不代表退出和加入的实际成本相同。如果订阅一键加入、取消却要六个页面和客服电话，choice 在形式上存在，但 friction 明显不对称。\n\n第三层接 measurement 与 causality。80% 用户保留默认方案，并不能直接证明他们有 status quo bias；他们也可能真的喜欢方案，或 switching cost 很高。同样，高 retention 也不一定等于高 satisfaction，它可能部分来自 cancellation friction。因此仍然要问 mechanism 和 counterfactual。\n\n第四层是 second-order effects：自动加入养老金可能提高 participation，却也可能让默认缴费比例变成 anchor，使一部分人少存钱。一个 default 改善一个指标，不代表整个系统都更优。\n\n今天加入 toolbox 的核心问题是 Who Benefits from Inaction? 面对自动续费、隐私权限、养老金、投资配置或软件设置时，依次问：如果我什么都不做会怎样？改变它需要多少 friction？默认值传递了什么 recommendation signal？设计者的 incentive 是什么？长期 second-order effect 又是什么？";


