  "title": "Why Cheap Products Can Become Expensive Habits",
  "cat": "Consumer Psychology",
  "framework": "Low Unit Price → Low Decision Salience → Frequent Repetition → Weak Memory → Large Cumulative Cost",
  "idea": "Consumers often evaluate small purchases one at a time, while their financial impact accumulates across time. Low prices reduce the psychological importance of each decision, so frequency and repetition can matter more than the cost of any single purchase."
