archivedArticles[52]="Imagine two investors making very different decisions. The first carefully studies a company, considers several scenarios, limits the size of the investment, and concludes that the expected return justifies the risk. Soon afterward, an unexpected political event causes the stock to fall sharply. The second investor buys another stock because a stranger online says it will rise. By chance, the price doubles. Who made the better decision?\n\nIf we judge only by outcomes, the answer seems obvious: the second investor made money and the first lost it. But this conclusion confuses decision quality with realized result. Under uncertainty, the two are related but not identical. A sensible decision can lead to a bad outcome, while a careless decision can be rewarded by luck.\n\nPsychologists call one version of this mistake outcome bias. Outcome bias occurs when people evaluate the quality of a past decision mainly according to what happened afterward, even when that result could not have been known at the time. Once we know the ending, it becomes difficult to reconstruct the uncertainty that existed before it.\n\nThis distinction depends on probability. Before a decision, we face a distribution of possible outcomes. Afterward, we observe only one realized outcome. The fact that one branch occurred does not erase the probability of the branches that did not. A 90 percent chance is not a promise. If an event with a 10 percent probability happens, the probability estimate was not necessarily wrong.\n\nThis connects to counterfactual thinking from #41. After a bad result, we easily imagine the better branch: “If only I had chosen differently.” Counterfactuals can help us learn, but they can also create an illusion that the alternative outcome was guaranteed. If you reject a job offer and your current company later struggles, it is tempting to imagine that accepting the other job would certainly have produced a better life. In reality, that path contained uncertainty too.\n\nHindsight bias makes the problem stronger. After learning an outcome, people often feel that it was more predictable than it actually was. Warning signs that once seemed ambiguous become obvious in retrospect. A failed company suddenly appears “clearly doomed”; a successful technology looks “inevitable.” The final result reorganizes our memory of the evidence.\n\nThis creates a learning problem. If we reward every decision that ends well and punish every decision that ends badly, people may learn the wrong lessons. A manager who takes an unreasonable risk and gets lucky may become more confident. Another manager who makes a careful decision but experiences an unlikely loss may become excessively cautious. Outcomes provide information, but without examining the process, feedback can reinforce bad behavior.\n\nThe solution is not to ignore outcomes. Results matter because they contain new evidence. If a strategy repeatedly fails, our model of the world may be wrong. The key word is repeatedly. One observation usually provides less information than a pattern across many comparable cases. This is a measurement issue: a single realized outcome is a noisy measure of the quality of a probabilistic decision.\n\nExpected value helps clarify the logic. Expected value combines possible outcomes with their probabilities. Suppose a decision has an 80 percent chance of gaining $100 and a 20 percent chance of losing $100. Its expected monetary value is positive: $60. If the loss occurs once, the realized return is negative, but the decision can still have been attractive before the uncertainty was resolved.\n\nOf course, expected value is not the only consideration. People have different risk tolerances, losses may have nonlinear consequences, and some outcomes cannot be reduced to money. A decision with positive expected value may still be inappropriate if the downside could destroy something essential. Good process therefore includes both probability and consequence.\n\nThis connects to #39's stock-versus-flow framework. A single gain or loss is a flow-like event, while decision skill is a stock that affects many future choices. Sacrificing good decision habits to avoid the emotional pain of one bad outcome can damage that stock. The goal is not to win every round; it is to preserve a process that performs well across repeated decisions.\n\nIt also connects to #50's calibration. A calibrated thinker does not say, “I was right because the event happened.” Instead, they ask whether events assigned high probabilities happen more often than events assigned low probabilities over time. Calibration requires a record of forecasts, not just memories of dramatic successes and failures.\n\nThis is why decision journals can be useful. Before making an important choice, write down the information available, the alternatives considered, the main assumptions, the expected benefits and risks, and what evidence would change your mind. Later, compare the outcome with the original reasoning. The journal prevents hindsight from quietly rewriting what you believed before the result was known.\n\nA decision journal also helps separate process errors from outcome noise. Perhaps the outcome was bad because an unlikely event occurred. That is outcome noise. Or perhaps you ignored relevant evidence, misunderstood an incentive, used a weak proxy, or failed to consider a realistic alternative. Those are process errors. Only the second category clearly demands a change in reasoning.\n\nCausality matters here as well. A good result does not prove that the action caused it. A company may launch a new advertising campaign just before sales rise, but perhaps the entire market was expanding. A student may adopt a new study technique before receiving a high score, but the exam may also have been easier. As in earlier lessons, visible sequence is not enough to identify mechanism.\n\nThere are second-order effects in organizations. If employees know they will be judged entirely by outcomes, they may avoid reasonable risks whose failures would look embarrassing. They may also hide uncertainty and present decisions as more certain than they really are. An outcome-only culture can therefore reduce experimentation and encourage defensive decision-making.\n\nThe opposite extreme is also dangerous. “The process was good” should not become an excuse that protects people from evidence forever. A process deserves trust only if it is updated when enough results contradict its assumptions. Good decision-making requires both process accountability and empirical feedback.\n\nToday's transferable tool is the Process-Outcome Split. After any important result, make two evaluations. First, judge the decision using only information that was reasonably available beforehand: Was the objective clear? Were relevant alternatives considered? Were probabilities and consequences treated seriously? Second, use the outcome as new evidence: What did it teach us about our assumptions?\n\nThis creates a healthier relationship with uncertainty. Instead of asking only, “Did I win?”, ask, “Was the bet sensible given what I knew?” And instead of asking only, “Was my reasoning sensible?”, ask, “What should the result now change?” The first question protects us from outcome bias. The second protects us from stubbornness.\n\nA mature decision system needs both. We cannot control which branch of uncertainty becomes reality, but we can improve how we choose among branches before the answer is known. Good decisions do not guarantee good outcomes. Their value is that, across many uncertain situations, they make good outcomes more likely.";
archivedSummaries[52]="今天把一个很容易被结果“骗过去”的问题拆开：Good Decision 和 Good Outcome 不是同一个概念。只要世界里存在 uncertainty，一个高质量决策就可能遇到低概率坏结果；一个随意决策也可能因为 luck 获得漂亮结果。\n\n核心链条是：Information Available → Decision Process → Probability Distribution → Realized Outcome → Separate Evaluation。决策发生时，我们面对的是一整组 possible outcomes；事后却只看到其中一个 realized outcome。Outcome bias 会让我们拿事后结果倒推事前决策质量，而 hindsight bias 又会让已经发生的结果显得“早就很明显”。\n\n这里最重要的 measurement distinction 是：single outcome 是一个 noisy measure。一次亏损不能自动证明策略差，一次盈利也不能自动证明策略好。Expected value 帮我们把 probability 重新放回分析；同时还要考虑 consequence，因为正期望值也可能包含无法承受的 downside。\n\n文章接回 #41 counterfactual：事后想象另一条路时，我们容易把“另一条路也有不确定性”忘掉；接 #50 calibration：真正的 calibration 需要长期比较预测概率和实际频率，而不是用一次结果宣布“我预测对了”；接 stock vs flow：单次输赢更像 flow，而 decision skill 是会影响未来大量选择的 stock。\n\n今天的新工具是 Process-Outcome Split。复盘时强制做两次评价：第一遍只使用事前合理可得的信息，判断 process 是否清楚、是否考虑 alternatives/probability/consequence；第二遍再把 outcome 当作 new evidence，问它应该如何更新 assumptions。这样既避免“结果差=决策差”，也避免用“过程没问题”拒绝现实反馈。\n\n最终原则是：Judge the decision by what was knowable then; use the outcome to learn what is knowable now。";


